@runbook-step-persistence
Feature: Runbook and Step Persistence
  As the Forge output-side loop
  I want runbooks and their ordered steps persisted durably in the build store
  So that a build's step-by-step progress survives a crash and can be resumed from where it left off

  Background:
    Given the Forge runbook store has been initialised

  # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # GROUP A — Key Examples
  # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  # Why: Core write path — a runbook with its ordered steps is persisted (create_runbook)
  @task:TASK-RSP-003
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Creating a runbook persists it with its ordered steps
    Given a runbook for a target with three ordered steps
    When I create the runbook in the store
    Then the runbook should be persisted under its runbook identifier
    And all three steps should be stored in their original order
    And the create call should report success

  # Why: Core read path — a persisted runbook is loaded back faithfully (load_runbook)
  @task:TASK-RSP-003
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Loading a persisted runbook returns it with all its steps
    Given a runbook with three ordered steps has been created for a target
    When I load the runbook by its identifier
    Then the loaded runbook should report the same target name and creation time
    And its steps should be returned in sequence order
    And its resume pointer and overall status should match what was stored

  # Why: Core write path — a single step's status is updated durably and is queryable per step (update_step_status)
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=medium] A step is addressed by its sequence position within the runbook (no standalone step id).
  @task:TASK-RSP-004
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Updating a step status is recorded and queryable for that step
    Given a created runbook whose steps are all pending
    When I mark the second step as passed
    And I reload the runbook
    Then the second step should be reported as passed
    And the other steps should remain pending

  # Why: Core write path — the resume pointer advances to the next step (advance)
  @task:TASK-RSP-004
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Advancing a runbook moves the resume pointer to the next step
    Given a created runbook whose resume pointer is at the first step
    When I advance the runbook
    And I reload the runbook
    Then the resume pointer should point at the second step

  # Why: Initial state — a freshly created runbook is positioned at its first pending step
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=medium] The resume pointer starts at the first step on create.
  @task:TASK-RSP-003
  @key-example
  Scenario: A newly created runbook starts at its first step with every step pending
    Given a runbook with three ordered steps
    When I create the runbook in the store
    And I load it back
    Then every step should be pending
    And the resume pointer should point at the first step

