  # Why: the write lock plus the identifier conflict must serialise rival creates so one wins whole and the other is refused, never a partial runbook
  @task:TASK-RSP-006
  @edge-case @negative
  Scenario: Two simultaneous creations of the same runbook leave exactly one whole runbook
    Given the runbook store is empty
    And two writers each prepare to create a runbook under the identifier "rb-clash" with three ordered steps
    When both writers attempt to create that runbook at the same time
    Then exactly one creation succeeds
    And the other creation is refused as a duplicate identifier
    And loading the runbook "rb-clash" returns it with all three steps intact
    And no half-written step is left behind by the refused creation

  # Why: a concurrent advance and a status update must serialise under the write lock so both the moved pointer and the recorded status survive
  @task:TASK-RSP-006
  @edge-case
  Scenario: An advance and a step status update on one runbook serialise without lost work
    Given a runbook "rb-serial" is persisted with three pending steps and the resume pointer on the first step
    And one writer is set to advance the runbook to the next step
    And another writer is set to mark the first step as passed
    When both writers act on the runbook at the same time
    Then both operations succeed
    And loading the runbook shows the resume pointer on the second step
    And loading the runbook shows the first step as passed

  # Why: a read-only reader sees only committed state, so an in-flight result write is all-or-nothing, never a half-written step
  @task:TASK-RSP-006
  @edge-case
  Scenario: A reader sees a consistent snapshot while a step result is being recorded
    Given a runbook "rb-snapshot" is persisted with a running first step
    And a reader opens the runbook store for reading only
    When a writer begins recording a result with exit code 0 and captured output for the first step
    And the reader loads the runbook before that recording is committed
    Then the reader observes the first step without the new result
    When the recording is committed and the reader loads the runbook again
    Then the reader observes the first step with exit code 0 and its captured output
    And the reader never observes a half-written step

